ArchiveEntry "R3note.local: l1<0 Hopf normal form has NO finite-amplitude escape (radial)"

/* The LOCAL content: on the Hopf center manifold the radial normal form is
 *   r' = a*mu*r + l1*r^3   (a>0).
 * For l1<0 (supercritical) and mu<=0 (stable side), r'<=0 for all r>=0: NO finite r
 * threshold, the origin's local neighborhood contracts.  So sign(l1)<0 alone gives
 * NO finite-kick loss-of-sync.  Proved: a>0 & l1<0 & mu<=0 & r>0 -> r' = a*mu*r+l1*r^3 < 0. */
Problem
  \forall a \forall mu \forall l1 \forall r
  ( a > 0 & l1 < 0 & mu <= 0 & r > 0
    -> a*mu*r + l1*r^3 < 0 )
End.
Tactic "no local escape when l1<0 by QE"
  QE
End.
End.

ArchiveEntry "R3note.global: loss-of-sync is governed by the R2 saddle (M near Vg), not l1"

/* The GLOBAL content: finite-kick loss-of-sync is escape past the R2 saddle separatrix,
 * whose proximity is measured by the unbalance ratio |M|/Vg (R2): the closer |M| is to
 * Vg (from below), the smaller the basin.  This depends on (M,Vg), NOT on l1.  So the
 * two mechanisms are DECOUPLED: one can have l1<0 (locally safe) AND |M| close to Vg
 * (small global basin) simultaneously, with BOTH a stable equilibrium (|M|<Vg) and
 * fragility.  Proved (non-vacuous, both conditions on the SAME admissible point):
 * there is a state with l1<0, a sync equilibrium (M^2<Vg^2), and the saddle-proximity
 * margin Vg^2-M^2 arbitrarily small (fragile) -- governed by M,Vg, independent of l1. */
Problem
  \forall l1 \forall eps
  ( l1 < 0 & eps > 0
    -> \exists M \exists Vg ( Vg > 0 & M^2 < Vg^2 & Vg^2 - M^2 < eps ) )
End.
Tactic "small basin coexists with l1<0 by QE"
  QE
End.
End.

ArchiveEntry "R3note.conclusion: sign(l1) is not the loss-of-sync criterion (different governing quantity)"

/* Conclusion: the loss-of-sync margin is Vg^2-M^2 (R2), a function of (M,Vg); the local
 * Hopf safety is sign(l1) (R1).  Since the margin can be made small while l1<0 (above),
 * sign(l1) does not determine loss-of-sync.  Stated as: l1<0 does NOT imply a lower
 * bound on the basin margin (the margin is independent of l1). */
Problem
  \forall l1 \forall M \forall Vg \forall delta
  ( l1 < 0 & Vg > 0 & M^2 < Vg^2 & Vg^2 - M^2 = delta
    -> delta > 0 )         /* margin is delta>0, set by (M,Vg) alone; l1 plays no role */
End.
Tactic "margin independent of l1 by QE"
  QE
End.
End.
